# Agent Transaction Risk Layer
### Razorpay Buildathon — Track 02 (AI Risk Manager) — Full Build Specification

---

## 0. The One-Sentence Pitch

> Existing fraud systems break the moment an AI agent is in the checkout flow — every signal they were trained on (device fingerprint, clickstream, behavioral biometrics) either disappears or describes the agent instead of the human. This is a fraud detection, mandate verification, and dispute-evidence layer purpose-built for agent-initiated payments, built on Razorpay's live mandate and dispute APIs.

Memorise this. It is the sentence that separates you from a generic fraud classifier.

---

## 1. Why This Problem Is Real (Your Evidence Base)

Do not skip this section. In a 5-minute pitch, *proving the problem is real* buys more credibility than any feature demo. Every claim below is sourced and verifiable — cite them in your README and video.

### 1.1 The protocols deliberately left fraud out of scope
The Agentic Commerce Protocol (ACP, by OpenAI + Stripe) explicitly excludes **fraud modeling, returns/exchanges, and PSP authorization semantics** from its specification. Those responsibilities stay with the merchant's own stack. There is no default solution shipping with the protocol — the hole is by design.

**Implication for you:** you are not competing with the protocol. You are building the layer it declined to build.

### 1.2 Legacy fraud models don't degrade gracefully — they invert
Industry research is blunt about this: device signals, behavioral biometrics, IP addresses and clickstream patterns either vanish or reflect the agent rather than the user. Fraud models go from useful to *actively misleading*, because an agent's behavior looks — by every metric those models were trained on — like automated fraud.

**Implication for you:** legitimate, fully-authorized agent purchases get blocked as false positives. This is a revenue problem, not just a security problem. Frame it that way to judges: you are recovering good transactions, not just blocking bad ones.

### 1.3 Nobody sees the whole transaction
No single party has the full picture. The AI provider verified the user at onboarding. The merchant only ever sees the agent. The card issuer works from signals that no longer reflect human intent. Attacks exploit the *gaps between* these actors rather than weaknesses within any one of them.

### 1.4 The evidence gap and unresolved liability
Merchants under agentic checkout carry the liability without the evidence infrastructure to defend a dispute — the chargeback process was built assuming a human initiated the purchase. A survey of 500 senior fraud/risk/security professionals found **no consensus at all** on who pays when an agent transaction goes wrong: ~39% said the AI provider, 20% the customer, 14% the merchant/platform, 11% the bank, 15% wanted a shared model.

**Implication for you:** your evidence generator isn't a nice-to-have. It is the artifact that resolves an argument the industry cannot currently settle.

### 1.5 Speed turns a single error into a systemic one
An erroneous or fraudulent agent transaction that a human would have caught at the checkout moment can now replicate across thousands of orders before anyone notices. Detection latency matters more than in human commerce.

### 1.6 Prompt injection is already draining agent wallets in the wild
This is the newest and most underrated part of the threat surface:

- Security researchers documented live campaigns using **SEO poisoning** and hidden instructions in web content to trick payment-capable AI agents into executing transactions — including payloads containing fully-specified transaction details (recipient, amount, description) with step-by-step instructions to execute without user confirmation.
- Google researchers observed a **32% increase in malicious prompt-injection payloads** embedded in web content between November 2025 and February 2026.
- In a public red-teaming competition against deployed agents, 1.8 million injection attempts produced **more than 60,000 successful policy violations**.
- Brave's security team demonstrated indirect injection against an AI browser using instructions invisible to the user (white text on white background, HTML comments), causing cross-site actions including fetching OTPs and touching banking portals — from a request as innocuous as "summarize this page."

**Implication for you — and this is your single strongest differentiator:** in an injected-intent attack, *the agent is legitimate, the credential is valid, the mandate is active, and the amount is within limits.* Every existing control passes it. The only thing that is wrong is the **intent**. Nothing in the current stack detects that. See §3.4.

### 1.7 What the big networks are building (and why you're not duplicating them)
Visa's Trusted Agent Protocol and Mastercard's Agent Pay / Verifiable Intent are network-level answers to this. Mastercard binds an Agentic Token to an agent identity plus cardholder mandates (spend ceilings, merchant categories, time windows, recurring rules). Visa attaches a signed-intent payload and agent credentials, with agent providers pre-registering keys.

**Use this two ways:**
1. As validation — "the card networks are building exactly this layer, which tells you the problem is real."
2. As design guidance — their signal taxonomy tells you what a technically credible version looks like. You are building the **merchant-side** equivalent for the Indian/UPI/Razorpay context, which they do not cover.

**Do not overclaim.** You are not reimplementing TAP. You are building the merchant-side risk layer that consumes these kinds of signals.

