## 3. The Four Layers — Full Feature Detail

### 3.1 Layer 1 — Mandate Verifier (deterministic)

**Razorpay primitives to build on:** the TPAP Pro Mandate APIs give you create / update / revoke / pause / resume / approve / reject / fetch. Mandates carry an amount, an amount rule (`max` vs `exact`), a Unique Mandate Number (UMN), payer and payee details, device details, and timestamps.

**The core abstraction — state this explicitly in your README and pitch:**
> Razorpay has no first-class "AI agent" object. I model an agent's delegated authority using the **mandate** primitive: a mandate is a scoped, revocable, user-approved grant of spending authority — structurally the same thing Mastercard's Agentic Token and NPCI's UAP consent model express. Every check below runs against that grant.

Being upfront about this is a strength. A judge who spots an unexplained gap will doubt you; a judge who sees you name the abstraction and justify it will credit you for systems thinking.

**Checks to implement:**

| # | Check | What it catches | Data source |
|---|---|---|---|
| V1 | Amount vs. mandate ceiling (respecting `max` vs `exact`) | Over-limit execution | Mandate amount + rule |
| V2 | Merchant / category scope | Agent buying outside permitted categories | Payee details vs. allowed set |
| V3 | Time-window validity | Off-schedule execution | Mandate start/end timestamps |
| V4 | Lifecycle state (active / paused / revoked / rejected) | Use of a revoked or paused grant | Fetch Mandates API |
| V5 | UMN integrity & uniqueness | Malformed or duplicated mandate references | UMN field |
| V6 | Signature / key attestation (simulated) | Spoofed agent identity | Your simulated agent key registry |
| V7 | Cumulative spend against mandate budget | Slow drain via many small in-limit charges | Your own ledger |
| V8 | Cadence vs. recurring rule | Higher frequency than authorized | Mandate rule + history |

**Critical output design:** emit a **structured reason trail**, not a log line. Something like:

```json
{
  "verdict": "fail",
  "failed_checks": ["V1", "V7"],
  "evidence": {
    "V1": {"txn_amount": 4200, "mandate_ceiling": 2000, "rule": "max"},
    "V7": {"cumulative_spend": 18400, "budget": 20000, "window": "2026-08"}
  },
  "mandate_snapshot": {"umn": "...", "state": "active", "granted_at": "..."}
}
```

Build this on day one. Layer 4 consumes it directly, and reason trails are what make the whole system explainable — a huge scoring advantage over a black-box classifier.

---

### 3.2 Layer 2 — Behavioral Detector (ML — your precision/recall centerpiece)

The track brief asks for *"measured precision and recall on a held-out test set."* This layer produces that number.

**The insight that makes this novel:** you cannot use human behavioral signals — the research says plainly they're gone or misleading. So model the **session shape** instead: the sequence, timing and structure of API calls, not the human behind them.

**Feature families:**

**A. Sequence features**
- N-gram profile of the API call sequence (`discover → cart → consent → pay` vs. anomalous orderings)
- Skip-ahead detection: payment without a preceding cart/consent step
- Sequence edit-distance from that mandate's own historical modal path
- Retry/backoff structure — real agents retry with backoff, scripts often don't
- Abandoned-step ratio

**B. Timing features**
- Inter-step latency distribution (mean, variance, min)
- **Variance is the money feature:** genuine LLM agents have *irregular* latency because inference time varies with context; replay scripts are suspiciously uniform. Low variance is a strong tell.
- Total session duration vs. cart complexity
- Time-of-day plausibility against the mandate's own history

**C. Credential / token features**
- Consent-token reuse across distinct sessions (replay)
- Token age at time of use
- Key-rotation anomalies
- Same UMN appearing from divergent device fingerprints

**D. Velocity & drift features**
- Transactions per mandate per hour/day vs. authorized cadence
- Burst detection (n transactions in a short window)
- Per-mandate personalized baseline drift — z-score against that mandate's *own* history, not a global threshold. Personalized baselines materially outperform global rules and are worth calling out.
- Basket-composition drift (sudden shift in categories or item mix)

**E. Cross-entity graph features (stretch — high payoff)**
- Mandates sharing a device fingerprint or signing key
- Connected-component size in the mandate↔device↔key graph
- Velocity of *new mandate creation* per device — the coordinated-ring signal

**Modeling approach — be pragmatic:**
1. **Baseline:** gradient-boosted trees (XGBoost / LightGBM) on aggregated features. Fast, strong, interpretable. Ship this first.
2. **Sequence model (if time permits):** a small LSTM/GRU or transformer over the raw call sequence. Only attempt after the baseline works end-to-end.
3. **Unsupervised companion:** Isolation Forest for novel attack classes you didn't simulate. Real-world value here: you cannot label attacks that don't exist yet.

**Reporting — do this properly, it's your credibility anchor:**
- Precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC (not just ROC-AUC — your classes are imbalanced, and knowing that distinction signals you understand the domain)
- Per-attack-class breakdown — which classes you catch well and which you don't
- **Threshold justification with an explicit cost model.** A false positive blocks a legitimate customer's agent; a false negative loses money. State your assumed rupee cost for each and show the threshold that minimizes total expected loss. Very few student projects do this, and it reads as commercially literate.
- A confusion matrix at your chosen operating point
- Feature importance (SHAP if you can) — ties back to explainability

**Non-negotiable:** freeze the held-out split *before* you start modeling, and never touch it until final evaluation. If a judge suspects leakage, every number you present dies.

