## 4. Data Simulation Plan

Everything depends on this being credible. Weak simulation → meaningless precision/recall.

**Scope it to a quick-commerce merchant** (grocery / food delivery). NPCI has publicly indicated the first agentic use cases will be low-value high-frequency — groceries, routine bills — and Razorpay's own live agentic pilot ran with Zomato, Swiggy and Zepto. Matching that context ties your work to their actual roadmap.

**Scale:** ~500 mandates, ~50,000 transactions, roughly 2–4% attack prevalence. Realistic imbalance matters — a 50/50 split makes your metrics meaningless and a judge will notice immediately.

**Legitimate traffic must be genuinely varied**, or your model learns "anything unusual = fraud":
- Different agent types with different latency profiles
- Weekday/weekend and time-of-day rhythms
- Basket size variation, seasonal spikes, legitimate retries after network failures
- Legitimate mandate updates and user-initiated pauses

**The six attack classes:**

| # | Class | Layer that should catch it |
|---|---|---|
| A1 | Consent-token replay | L2 (token reuse, timing uniformity) |
| A2 | Spoofed agent identity | L1 (V6 attestation) |
| A3 | Over-ceiling burst | L1 (V1, V7) |
| A4 | Off-window / revoked-mandate use | L1 (V3, V4) |
| A5 | Slow drain — many small in-limit charges | L2 (velocity, cumulative drift) |
| A6 | **Injected intent — everything valid, goal hijacked** | **L3 only** |

A6 is the demo that wins the room: show Layers 1 and 2 both passing it cleanly, then Layer 3 catching it. That single visual makes the case for the entire project.

**Also simulate hard negatives** — legitimate transactions that superficially resemble attacks (a genuine bulk festival order, a legitimate new-beneficiary purchase). Your false-positive rate is only meaningful if the negatives are actually hard.

**Reproducibility:** seed your RNG, version the generator, commit the config. A judge should be able to regenerate your dataset exactly.

