## 11. Making It Read as Enterprise-Grade

More detection features do not make a project enterprise-grade. A payments company judges "enterprise-grade" on a completely different axis: **can this survive contact with real money, real attackers, and real auditors?** That's mostly invisible in a demo unless you deliberately surface it. You cannot fully *build* all of this in a month as a solo builder — and trying to would sink your core features. What you can do is **implement a thin, real slice of each category and document the rest as a designed-for roadmap.** That combination — some working, all designed — is what actually reads as senior thinking to a judge who's shipped production payment systems.

### 11.1 Security & compliance posture

- **Secrets management** — Razorpay API keys never in code or committed config. Use environment variables or a vault; `.env.example` in the repo, real `.env` gitignored. This is a one-line fix that a surprising number of student submissions get wrong, and it's the first thing a technical judge checks.
- **Least privilege** — your service should hold only the Razorpay scopes it needs (disputes, mandates), not a full-access key. State this explicitly even if your test setup doesn't enforce it.
- **PII minimization** — decide up front what customer/agent data you actually need to store vs. what you can hash or drop. Document a retention policy, even a simple one ("session logs purged after 90 days").
- **Encryption posture** — state your assumption (TLS in transit, encryption at rest via your DB provider) even if you didn't build custom crypto. Judges want to see you know the requirement exists.
- **Regulatory awareness** — one paragraph in your README mapping your system to the actual frameworks it would need to respect in production: RBI's data localization and payment aggregator guidelines, and PCI-DSS if card data ever touches your layer. You don't need compliance certification — you need to show you know it's coming.

### 11.2 Reliability & observability

- **Structured logging** — every decision from every layer emitted as structured JSON (not print statements), so it's queryable. This single choice makes your evidence packets (§3.4) trivial instead of hand-built.
- **Idempotency keys** on any state-changing call (pausing a mandate, contesting a dispute) — prevents duplicate actions on retry, which matters enormously once real money is involved.
- **Retries with exponential backoff + circuit breakers** around your Razorpay API calls — a payments system that falls over when an upstream API blips is not enterprise-grade, it's a script.
- **A stated latency budget per layer** — e.g., "Layer 1 must resolve in <50ms, Layer 2 in <200ms, since checkout can't hang." You don't have to hit real production numbers, but naming the budget shows you understand payments is latency-sensitive in a way batch ML isn't.
- **Basic metrics/dashboarding** — even a simple counts-and-latency panel (verdicts per layer, false-positive rate over time) signals you're thinking about *running* the system, not just training a model once.

### 11.3 Testing rigor beyond the ML metrics

- **Unit tests** for each verifier check (V1–V8) — deterministic logic is exactly the kind of thing that should never regress silently.
- **An adversarial test suite for Layer 3** — a small, versioned set of known injection patterns (drawn from the documented campaigns you already cited) that must always be caught. This is your regression net against the most novel part of your system, and having one at all is rare for a student project.
- **A model regression check** — before any retrain, the new model must not underperform the old one on the frozen held-out set. Even a simple assert-based check communicates you understand model rollback risk.
- **Chaos-lite testing** — deliberately feed the pipeline malformed mandate data, a missing field, an out-of-order webhook. Show it fails safely (denies/flags) rather than crashing or silently approving. In payments, *fail closed* is the correct default and stating that explicitly is a real signal.

### 11.4 Model governance

- **Versioning** — every model artifact tagged with the data snapshot and feature-set version that produced it. Untraceable models are a real production liability and judges with ML backgrounds will ask about this.
- **Drift monitoring design** — you don't need live production traffic to implement this, but describe how you'd detect that agent behavior has shifted enough that the model needs retraining (e.g., population stability index on your key features).
- **A retraining cadence and rollback plan** — one paragraph is enough: what triggers a retrain, who/what approves promoting a new model, how you'd roll back a regression.
- **Fairness/consistency check across attack classes** — confirm your model isn't achieving good aggregate metrics by ignoring one attack class entirely (this is a common, judge-visible failure mode — always show the per-class breakdown from §3.2, not just an aggregate number).

### 11.5 API and platform design

- **Versioned API surface** for your own service (`/v1/...`), even if you're the only consumer — shows you're building a platform, not a script.
- **Multi-tenancy boundary** — if this served multiple merchants, mandate data and risk models must not leak across tenants. State the isolation model (per-merchant namespace, at minimum) even if your demo only has one simulated merchant.
- **Rate limiting on your own endpoints** — one sentence acknowledging this exists as a requirement is enough at hackathon scale.
- **Auth on your console** — don't leave the merchant dashboard open with no login. Even a minimal auth layer avoids an obvious "this couldn't ship" objection.

### 11.6 Documentation as an artifact, not an afterthought

Enterprise systems are judged partly on whether someone *other than the author* could operate them. Add:

- **A runbook** — "if the false-positive rate spikes, do X; if Layer 3 latency degrades, do Y." Even three entries signals operational maturity.
- **An incident-response sketch** — if a rogue-agent attack gets through, what's the response sequence? Detect → contain (pause mandate) → notify → post-mortem. This maps directly onto real fintech incident processes.
- **A decision log** — a short file recording key design tradeoffs and why you made them (e.g., "chose GBM over a sequence model for Layer 2 because interpretability mattered more than marginal recall gain at this data volume"). This is one of the highest-signal, lowest-effort additions you can make — it shows judgment, not just output.

### 11.7 What to actually implement vs. what to document

Be deliberate about this split — attempting all of it will wreck your core detection work:

| Implement for real | Document as designed-for |
|---|---|
| Secrets via env vars, no hardcoded keys | Full RBI/PCI-DSS compliance mapping |
| Structured JSON logging | Live drift-monitoring dashboard |
| Idempotency keys on state-changing calls | Multi-region failover |
| Unit tests on Layer 1 checks | Full auth/RBAC system |
| Adversarial regression suite for Layer 3 | Production retraining pipeline automation |
| Fail-closed behavior on malformed input | Horizontal autoscaling |
| A one-page runbook and decision log | SOC2-style audit trail |

A judge from a payments company will recognize this split immediately, and recognize it *favorably* — it's exactly how real engineering teams scope a v1. Claiming you built full enterprise infrastructure in a month, when you obviously didn't, reads as less credible than honestly showing you know what production would require and made deliberate, stated tradeoffs to ship a working core in the time you had.